                title='Hands-On Agentic AI with LangGraph: Building Sequential, Parallel, and Conditional State Workflows',
                slug='hands-on-agentic-ai-langgraph-sequential-parallel-conditional-workflows',
                excerpt='My practice journey building stateful AI agent workflows — from BMI calculators and parallel UPSC essay graders to quadratic equation solvers and sentiment routers.',
                content='![Agentic AI LangGraph Hero](agentic-hero)\n\n# Moving Beyond Simple AI Chains\n\nWhen I first started learning AI development, everything was a linear chain: `Input -> Prompt -> LLM -> Output`. \n\nLinear chains work fine for simple questions, but they break down when you need complex reasoning, decision making, or loops. Real-world tasks need conditional branching (if/else), parallel processing (running 3 tasks at once), and self-correction loops (checking work and fixing errors).\n\nTo master stateful AI workflows, I built a hands-on practice repository: **`Agnetic_AI`**. Using **LangChain** and **LangGraph**, I wrote small, focused scripts to practice sequential, parallel, and conditional state graphs.\n\nHere is what I built and learned in each module.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Sequential Workflows (`Sequential Workflows/`)\n\nSequential workflows execute nodes one after another, passing a shared state object dictionary (`AgentState`) along the pipeline.\n\n```text\n[Input Data] ──► [Node 1: Compute / Process] ──► [Node 2: Generate / Summarize] ──► [Final State]\n```\n\nWhat I built in this module:\n- **`1_bmi_workflow.py`**: A state-based BMI calculator graph. It takes height and weight, computes the BMI score, categorizes the health range, and exports a visual chart of the graph flow.\n- **`2_llm_workflow.py`**: A clean node connection script linking `ChatMistralAI` to process queries sequentially.\n- **`3_prompt_workflow.py`**: A multi-step generation workflow. Node 1 creates an outline for a given topic; Node 2 receives the outline and writes the complete article.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Parallel Workflows & Fan-Out/Fan-In (`Parallel Workflows/`)\n\nParallel workflows run multiple tasks concurrently, then fan-in to merge results into a final summary. This speeds up execution and keeps concerns separated.\n\n```text\n                     ┌──► Node A: Strike Rate ─────────┐\n                     │                                 │\n[Player Stats] ──────┼──► Node B: Boundary % ──────────┼──► [Merge & Summary Node]\n                     │                                 │\n                     └──► Node C: Balls per Boundary ──┘\n```\n\nWhat I built in this module:\n- **`1_batsman_workflow.py`**: A cricket stats calculator graph. It computes strike rate, boundary percentage, and balls per boundary across 3 parallel branches simultaneously before merging them into a player summary.\n- **`2_UPSC_essay_workflow.py`**: An essay evaluation system. Three parallel worker nodes inspect language quality, analytical depth, and clarity of thought at the same time using structured outputs (`Pydantic`), fanning in to combine scores and generate an overall feedback report.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Conditional Workflows & Dynamic Routing (`Conditional Workflows/`)\n\nConditional workflows use router functions to inspect the current state and dynamically decide which path to take next.\n\n```text\n                        ┌──► [Real Roots Node]\n[Discriminant Calculation] ──┤\n                        ├──► [Repeating Roots Node]\n                        └──► [Complex Roots Node]\n```\n\nWhat I built in this module:\n- **`1_quadratic_equation_workflow.py`**: A solver for quadratic equations ($ax^2 + bx + c = 0$). It computes the discriminant ($b^2 - 4ac$) and uses conditional edges (`add_conditional_edges`) to route execution to real roots, repeating roots, or complex numbers calculation nodes.\n- **`2_review_reply_workflow.py`**: Customer review automation. A router node analyzes sentiment: positive reviews route to a thank-you generator, while negative reviews route to a customer support escalation node.\n\n---\n\n## Key Lessons Learned\n\n1. **State Reducers Prevent Data Loss**: By using `Annotated[list, operator.add]`, LangGraph appends new messages to conversation lists instead of overwriting existing data.\n2. **Visual Debugging Helps**: Saving graph visualizations (`graph.get_graph().draw_png()`) made it super easy to verify routing logic before running live LLM calls.\n3. **Keep State Simple**: Storing clean, typed objects (`TypedDict` or `Pydantic`) makes state state changes predictable across nodes.\n\nAll code files, scripts, and workflow diagrams are available in my GitHub repository: [`shlokbam/Agnetic_AI`](https://github.com/shlokbam/Agnetic_AI).\n',
