                title='My LangSmith Learning Log: Tracing, Debugging, and Optimizing LLM Chains and RAG Pipelines',
                slug='my-langsmith-learning-log-tracing-debugging-optimizing-llm-chains-rag',
                excerpt='How I used LangSmith to peek inside AI chains — tracking prompts, latency, token costs, and optimizing a PDF RAG pipeline across 4 iterations.',
                content='![LangSmith LLM Observability Hero](langsmith-hero)\n\n# Why I Started Using LangSmith\n\nWhen an AI chain fails, hallucinates, or takes 10 seconds to respond, trying to guess what happened by reading console print statements is painful.\n\n**LangSmith** acts like a developer dashboard for LLM applications. It automatically records every prompt sent to an AI model, the raw output returned, token usage, latency, and nested execution steps.\n\nTo learn how to trace, debug, and optimize AI applications, I created a hands-on log repository: **`LangSmith`**. I built 5 distinct exercises progressing from simple LLM calls to multi-step RAG pipelines.\n\n---\n\n## My Practice Exercises & Experiments\n\n```text\n  Exercise 1               Exercise 2               Exercise 3 to 6\n┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────────┐        ┌───────────────────────┐\n│ Simple LLM   │ ─────► │ Sequential   │ ─────► │ PDF RAG Optimization  │\n│ Auto-Tracing │        │ Chain & Tags │        │ (islr.pdf v1 to v4)   │\n└──────────────┘        └──────────────┘        └───────────────────────┘\n```\n\n### 1. Simple LLM Call Tracing (`1_simple_llm_call.py`)\nI started by setting up auto-tracing via environment variables:\n\n```bash\n# Environment setup for automatic LangSmith tracing\nexport LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2="true"\nexport LANGCHAIN_ENDPOINT="https://api.smith.langchain.com"\nexport LANGCHAIN_API_KEY="your_langsmith_api_key_here"\nexport LANGSMITH_PROJECT="Simple LLM App"\n```\n\nRunning a basic `ChatMistralAI` invocation automatically sent a flat execution trace to my LangSmith dashboard showing the prompt template, model call, and output parser timing.\n\n### 2. Sequential Chains & Custom Metadata Tags (`2_sequential_chain.py`)\nNext, I built a sequential chain (`|` operator) that takes a topic (e.g., *"Unemployment in India"*), generates an in-depth report, and then feeds that report into a second prompt to create a 5-point summary.\n\nTo keep my dashboard organized, I attached custom tags (`[\'sequential\', \'llm\']`) and user metadata (`{\'user\': \'Shlok\', \'topic\': \'Unemployment in India\'}`). In the LangSmith UI, this rendered as a clear nested tree showing how data flowed from the first model call to the second.\n\n### 3. Optimizing a PDF RAG Pipeline Across 4 Versions (`3_rag_v1.py` to `3_rag_v4.py`)\nIn this experiment series, I built a RAG pipeline to answer questions from an academic textbook (`islr.pdf` — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning with Applications in R*):\n\n- **Version 1 (`3_rag_v1.py`)**: Ingested the PDF using `PyPDFLoader`, split text into chunks using `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter`, indexed vectors with `FAISS` and `MistralEmbeddings`, and ran basic queries.\n- **Using Traces to Improve Quality**: By looking at the trace logs in LangSmith, I realized that small chunk sizes were cutting off important statistical equations.\n- **Versions 2–4 (`v2` to `v4`)**: I tuned the chunk size, adjusted chunk overlap, and optimized prompt templates. Watching the latency and token count metrics in LangSmith gave me concrete data on how each tweak improved response accuracy.\n\n---\n\n## Main Takeaways\n\n1. **Zero-Code Tracing**: Enabling environment variables logs standard LangChain runs automatically without cluttering your code.\n2. **Span Trees Save Hours**: Being able to inspect exact prompt inputs and raw completion tokens makes fixing hallucinations much faster.\n3. **Watch Your Token Costs**: Seeing token counts per request helps you catch runaway context inflation before it turns into a expensive API bill.\n\nExplore all practice scripts, environment configs, and trace screenshots in my GitHub repository: [`shlokbam/LangSmith`](https://github.com/shlokbam/LangSmith).\n',
