                excerpt='A complete walkthrough of my hands-on practice repository — building RAG pipelines, testing text splitters, configuring ChromaDB vector stores, and mastering LCEL runnables.',
                content='![Generative AI LCEL Hero](genai-hero)\n\n# Overview of My Practice Repository\n\nGenerative AI is moving fast, and the best way to understand how document loaders, vector stores, and custom agents work is to build them yourself.\n\nI created a structured codebase: **`Generative_AI`** to practice and implement core concepts step by step. My repository covers everything from multi-format document ingestion and text splitting to **ChromaDB** vector storage and **LangChain Expression Language (LCEL)** runnables.\n\nHere is a tour of what I built across the three main modules.\n\n---\n\n## Repository Structure & Module Breakdown\n\n```text\nGenerative_AI/\n├── RAG_Project/             # Document loaders, splitters & ChromaDB RAG\n├── Video_1/                 # Groq, HuggingFace embeddings & Streamlit chatbot UI\n└── Agents/                  # LCEL runnables, custom @tool scripts & ReAct agents\n```\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — Document Ingestion & Text Splitting Experiments (`RAG_Project/`)\n\nBuilding a good Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) system starts with getting your data in cleanly:\n\n### Document Loaders (`RAG_Project/document_loaders/`)\nI practiced loading data from multiple format sources:\n- `pdf.py`: Using `PyPDFLoader` to read research papers (`GRU.pdf`).\n- `test.py`: Using `TextLoader` for plain text notes (`notes.txt`).\n- `page.py`: Using `WebBaseLoader` to scrape and parse web URLs directly.\n\n### Text Splitting Experiments (`RAG_Project/Test_splitter/`)\nI wrote scripts to compare different text chunking strategies:\n- `char_split.py`: Testing `CharacterTextSplitter`.\n- `token_split.py`: Testing `TokenTextSplitter`.\n- `semantic_split.py`: Testing `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter`.\n\n**Key Observation**: `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` worked best because it respects paragraph and sentence boundaries, preventing cut-off text compared to basic character splitting.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — Vector Database Storage with ChromaDB (`Vector_Store/`)\n\nOnce text is split into chunks, it needs to be embedded and stored in a vector database:\n\n- **`Database.py` & `Vector_Store/db.py`**: Ingests document chunks, generates embeddings using `MistralEmbeddings`, and persists records into a local **ChromaDB** vector store.\n- **Retrieval Testing (`Retrievers/`)**:\n  - `Arixv.py`: Searching academic research papers via `ArxivRetriever`.\n  - `Mmr.py`: Comparing standard Similarity Search vs. **Maximal Marginal Relevance (MMR)** to reduce duplicate search results.\n  - `Multiquery.py`: Testing `MultiQueryRetriever` to generate multiple query variations for better search coverage.\n\n---\n\n## Part 3 — LCEL Runnables, Tools & Agents (`Agents/`)\n\nLangChain Expression Language (LCEL) uses the pipe operator (`|`) to make chain composition clean and readable:\n\n### Runnables (`Agents/Runnables/`)\n- `Sequence_runnables.py`: Basic sequential chain (`prompt | llm | parser`).\n- `parallel_runnables.py`: `RunnableParallel` for running multi-branch tasks concurrently.\n- `passthrough_runnables.py`: `RunnablePassthrough` for passing raw inputs downstream without modifications.\n\n### Tools & ReAct Agents (`Agents/Tools/`)\n- `custom_tool.py`: Creating custom tools using the `@tool` decorator.\n- `news_summarizer.py`: Combining `TavilySearchResults` with an LCEL summarization chain.\n- `Agent.py`: Building a full LangGraph ReAct agent equipped with weather and news tools.\n- `streamlit_app.py`: An interactive Streamlit web dashboard showing live agent reasoning steps.\n\n```python\n# Sample LCEL Pipe Syntax I Practiced\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\n\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_template("Explain {topic} in simple terms.")\nchain = prompt | llm | StrOutputParser()\n\n# Running the chain\n# response = chain.invoke({"topic": "Vector Embeddings"})\n```\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nBuilding this repository gave me a clear, practical understanding of how Generative AI apps work behind the scenes. All scripts, RAG ingestion pipelines, and Streamlit UIs are available in my GitHub repository: [`shlokbam/Generative_AI`](https://github.com/shlokbam/Generative_AI).\n',
